<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.4.2: Zero Exponent Rule</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01298</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.4.2: Zero Exponent Rule</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f0be5bac-2770-439d-9acd-6b160caa5c8c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00030">1. Complete the table by entering the values for a, b, and c below the table. Take
          advantage of any patterns you notice.</para>

      <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00001">
        <tgroup cols="6">
<tbody>
            <row>
              <entry><emphasis><m:math>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math></emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry> 4 </entry>
              <entry> 3 </entry>
              <entry> 2 </entry>
              <entry> 1 </entry>
              <entry> 0 </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><emphasis><m:math>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:msup>
                        <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      </m:msup>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math></emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry> 81 </entry>
              <entry> 27 </entry>
              <entry> a. _____ </entry>
              <entry> b. _____ </entry>
              <entry> c. _____ </entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>

      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00043">a. ________</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">


          <para id="para-00002">Compare your answer: </para>

          <para id="para-00003">9</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->


      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00044">b. ________</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">


          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer: </para>

          <para id="para-00006">3</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->


      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00045">c. ________</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">


          <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer: </para>

          <para id="para-00009">1</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->

      <para id="para-00050">2. Here are some equations. Find the solution to each equation using what you know about
          exponent rules. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00001">a. <m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">


          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer: </para>

          <para id="para-00012">0</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->


      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00004">b.<m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>12</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:msup>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    </m:msup>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>12</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">


          <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer: </para>

          <para id="para-00015">0</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->

      <para id="para-00051">3. Answer the following questions.</para>

      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00016">a. What is the value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            ?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">


          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer: </para>

          <para id="para-00018">For positive whole numbers, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.
            For this law to still hold with 0 as an exponent, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            needs to be equal to 1 because <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            .</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->


      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00019">b. What about <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            ?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">


          <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer: </para>

          <para id="para-00021">For positive whole numbers, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.
            For this law to still hold with 0 as an exponent, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            needs to be equal to 1 because <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            .</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>

    <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you
    will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <!--Start
        interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all
        have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the
        button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00008">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00022">We know, for example, that <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="prefix">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="postfix">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="prefix">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="postfix">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>. The grouping with
              parentheses does not affect the value of the expression.</para>

            <para id="para-00023">Is this true for exponents? That is, are the numbers <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="prefix">(</m:mo>
                        <m:msup>
                          <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                          <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                        </m:msup>
                        <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="postfix">)</m:mo>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="prefix">(</m:mo>
                      <m:msup>
                        <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                        <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                      </m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="postfix">)</m:mo>
                    </m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              equal? If not, which is bigger?</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">


            <para id="para-00024">Compare your answer: </para>

            <para id="para-00025">The two numbers are not even close! Since <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>243</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>, the number <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="prefix">(</m:mo>
                        <m:msup>
                          <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                          <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                        </m:msup>
                        <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="postfix">)</m:mo>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              is <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>243</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.
              On the other hand, <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="prefix">(</m:mo>
                      <m:msup>
                        <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                        <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                      </m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="postfix">)</m:mo>
                    </m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>15</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.
              The number <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>243</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              is much larger than <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>15</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              .</para>


          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
        interaction-->

        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00009">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00026">Which of the two would you choose as the meaning of the expression <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:msup>
                        <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                        <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                      </m:msup>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              written without parentheses?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">


            <para id="para-00027">Compare your answer: </para>

            <para id="para-00028">Typically, we define repeated exponents by working down the tower,
              e.g., <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:msup>
                        <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                        <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                      </m:msup>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="prefix">(</m:mo>
                        <m:msup>
                          <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                          <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                        </m:msup>
                        <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="postfix">)</m:mo>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.
              One reason for this is that evaluating up the tower, as in <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="prefix">(</m:mo>
                      <m:msup>
                        <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                        <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                      </m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="postfix">)</m:mo>
                    </m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>,
              can already be simplified by using algebra rules (in this case, to get <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>15</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              ).</para>

          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
        interaction-->
      </section>
    </section>

    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00011">
        <problem id="prob-00011">
          <para id="para-00029"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_5_4_2_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
    </note>

    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>

      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Zero as an Exponent</title>
        <para id="para-00031">Why is <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, but <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> is not
          the same as <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>?</para>
        <para id="para-00032">Answering this question will help us understand the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">zero exponent rule</term>.</para>
        <para id="para-00033">Exponents in general represent repeated multiplication, much like
          multiplication represents repeated addition. Let’s use that understanding to evaluate <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>:</para>
        <para id="para-00007"><m:math>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>8</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00010"><m:math>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>25</m:mn>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>125</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00036">8 and 125 are obviously very different, but the method to get to each
          answer was the same. If we rewrite it as a table, we can work backward to understand how <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> are
          both 1.</para>
        <para id="para-00037">Using <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and
          filling in the parts we have found results in Table 1:</para>

        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00002">
          <tgroup cols="5">
<tbody>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis><m:math>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math></emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> 0 </entry>
                <entry> 1 </entry>
                <entry> 2 </entry>
                <entry> 3 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis><m:math>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math></emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> </entry>
                <entry> 2 </entry>
                <entry> 4 </entry>
                <entry> 8 </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>

        <para id="para-00038">Repeating that process for <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>
          results in Table 2:</para>

        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00003">
          <tgroup cols="5">
<tbody>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis><m:math>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math></emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> 0 </entry>
                <entry> 1 </entry>
                <entry> 2 </entry>
                <entry> 3 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis><m:math>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math></emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> </entry>
                <entry> 5 </entry>
                <entry> 25 </entry>
                <entry> 125 </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>

        <para id="para-00039">Since exponents represent repeated multiplication in both tables,
          additional terms are found by multiplying again by the base. What if we worked backward?
          If you multiply to move to the right in the table, you can divide by the common ratio to
          work backward. Start with the 8, and work backward to fill in the table:</para>

        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00004">
          <tgroup cols="5">
<tbody>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis><m:math>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math></emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> 0 </entry>
                <entry> 1 </entry>
                <entry> 2 </entry>
                <entry> 3 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis><m:math>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math></emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      <m:mi>÷</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                      <m:mi>÷</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                      <m:mi>÷</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry> 8 </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>

        <para id="para-00040">Repeat the process for Table 2:</para>

        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00005">
          <tgroup cols="5">
<tbody>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis><m:math>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math></emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> 0 </entry>
                <entry> 1 </entry>
                <entry> 2 </entry>
                <entry> 3 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis><m:math>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math></emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                      <m:mi>÷</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                      <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>25</m:mn>
                      <m:mi>÷</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                      <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>125</m:mn>
                      <m:mi>÷</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                      <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>25</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry> 125 </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>

        <para id="para-00041">After you have worked backward in each table, you will see that <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> both
          equal 1. When 1 is the exponent, your answer is the same as the common ratio, so when you
          divide by the common ratio, you end up with 1 as your answer every time. This is an
          important rule that can save a lot of time.</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Zero as an Exponent</title>
    <exercise id="exer-00010">
            <problem id="prob-00010">

              <para id="para-00042">Find the value of each of the following:</para>
<list id="list-00001">
             <item><para id="para-00013"><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                      <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para></item>
              <item><para id="para-00034"><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mn>246</m:mn>
                      <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para></item>
              <item><para id="para-00035"><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="prefix">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                        <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                        <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                        <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                        <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                        <m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="postfix">)</m:mo>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para></item>
              </list>
              <para id="para-00046">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button
          to compare your work.</para>

            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00010">
              <para id="para-00047">Compare your answer: </para>

              <para id="para-00048">Here is how to use the zero exponent rule to determine the value
          of each:</para>

              <para id="para-00049">The answer to each expression is 1. If you want to work
          backward, it doesn’t matter how large the original number is. <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>÷</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1246</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>÷</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>1246</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>, and both
          expressions only contain a base and zero as exponent, so the answer is 1. For c, there is
          an expression inside the parentheses that you can evaluate first, but since it is still
          the base raised to a zero exponent, the answer will still be one.</para>

            </solution>
          </exercise></note>
      </section>
    </section>

  </content>
</document>